<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  flagRequestFilterKeyEvents is the whole point: it is what lets the service see
  the Essential Key go down and, more importantly, come back up. Window content
  retrieval is what lets the finished text be pasted into the focused field.

  typeNotificationStateChanged is how the app sees what is playing. A MediaStyle
  notification carries its own MediaSession.Token in EXTRA_MEDIA_SESSION, and
  that token is the only route to now-playing that does not require being a
  NotificationListenerService — which Play Protect hard-blocks on a sideloaded
  APK. See media/NowPlaying.kt.

  What is *not* here matters as much. Subscribing to typeWindowContentChanged,
  as this used to, makes the framework build and marshal an AccessibilityEvent
  for every scroll, animation and text change in every app on the phone, all
  day, to hand to a method that drops them. That is invisible in the app's own
  CPU time and expensive everywhere else. A notification being posted is a far
  rarer event, which is what makes this one affordable.

  flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows is gone for the same reason: it keeps the
  window hierarchy of every interactive window continuously computed, and is
  only needed by services that call getWindows(). Pasting uses findFocus(), for
  which canRetrieveWindowContent alone is enough.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeNotificationStateChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagRequestFilterKeyEvents"
    android:canRequestFilterKeyEvents="true"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="1000"
    android:settingsActivity="com.ishaan.essentialvoice.MainActivity"
    android:summary="@string/accessibility_summary" />
